@inject UserSession Session

<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        Your ledger stays with you: on your own devices, or on a server you run yourself. There's no LoonieBooks service
        holding anyone's finances.
        @if (Session.IsDesktop && Session.Storage == StorageLocation.Cloud)
        {
            @:Right now it's in LoonieBooks' database on the computer running the desktop app.
        }
        else if (Session.Storage == StorageLocation.Cloud)
        {
            @:Right now it's in this LoonieBooks server's database, because your account is allowed to store a ledger here.
        }
        else
        {
            @:Right now it's in this browser, on this device, and nowhere else.
        }
        The bar at the top of every page says which, and <a href="data">Your data</a> explains it.
    </p>

    <h3>The places a ledger can live</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The desktop app's database</strong> (recommended): a file on your Windows PC, shared by every browser on it and, if you
            turn sharing on, your household's other devices at home.</li>
        <li><strong>This browser</strong> (any LoonieBooks site, signed in or not): a private database inside the browser. It never
            reaches the server and nobody else can see it, but it belongs to this browser alone. Clearing the site's data, a
            private or incognito window, or another browser or device each start from an empty ledger.</li>
        <li><strong>Your own server's database</strong>: on a LoonieBooks server you host, the people its owner allows can sign in with
            Google and keep their ledger there, so it's the same on any device. Signing in without that permission still keeps
            the ledger in the browser.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Wherever it lives, statements, pasted text and pictures you import are read in your browser; only the transactions you
        import are saved. Only the last four digits of an account number are ever stored.
    </p>
    <p>
        To show prices, exchange rates and home values, LoonieBooks downloads public data from a few other sites (the Bank of
        Canada, stocktreemap.com, ownquity.com). Those downloads don't include your accounts, amounts or transactions. The
        <a href="@SiteInfo.PrivacyUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy page</a> lists each one, what it sends, and the
        few that send something only when you choose to.
    </p>

    <h3>Backups</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Download backup</span> saves the whole ledger in one file: accounts, transactions, categories, tags, tax choices,
            receipts and household. Keep it somewhere safe; it holds your financial history.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Restore from a backup…</span> adds what a backup holds to the ledger in use. It skips anything already there
            and never deletes, so restoring twice is harmless.</li>
        <li>That's also how to move a ledger: download it in one place, restore it in another.</li>
    </ul>
    @if (Session.Storage == StorageLocation.Browser)
    {
        <p class="help-caution">With the ledger in this browser, a backup is your only copy if the browser's data is lost. Download one regularly.</p>
    }

    <h3>Copying a browser ledger across</h3>
    <p>
        If you kept a ledger in a browser before using the desktop app's database or your own server, the copy button under
        storage on <a href="data">Your data</a> copies it across. Anything already there is kept, duplicates are skipped, and the
        browser's copy is left as it is.
    </p>

    <h3>Start over</h3>
    <p>
        <span class="help-ui">Delete everything…</span> removes the whole ledger where it's stored now: every account, transaction, receipt, tax
        choice and household member, so you can import again from scratch. It offers <span class="help-ui">Download backup first</span>, then
        asks you to type DELETE. It can't be undone.
    </p>
</div>
